      "title": "以服務設計藍圖探討高齡旅遊非正式學習接觸點：無形服務再設計之途徑",
      "title_en": "Mapping Informal Learning Across Senior Travel Touchpoints: A Service Design Blueprint Approach to Intangible Service Redesign",
      "abstract": "高齡化社會催生了對健康長壽與終身學習的深層關注。休閒旅遊提供了真實生活場域，讓長者在日常探索中實踐非正式學習，藉此維繫身心福祉與人際連結。然而，過往學界多將年長旅客預設為被動接受服務的消費者，忽略了動態情境中的人機互動、數位載具操作如何實質誘發自主學習機制。為解構此一盲點，本研究聚焦於不同旅遊型態下的學習機制運作。\n\n在理論建構上，本研究跨域串聯成人教育學的Knowles自我導向學習、Kolb經驗學習圈、McClusky餘力理論，與服務管理學，導入「服務設計藍圖」（Service Design Blueprint, SDB）作為核心分析工具。研究採質性多元個案研究法，深度訪談 10 位年齡介於 65 至 94 歲且經驗豐富的高齡旅行者；比對團體套裝旅遊（GIT）與散客自由行（FIT）在「行前、行中、行後」三階段的經驗軌跡，將其對應至交通、住宿、導覽等六大關鍵服務接觸點。\n\n實證分析顯示，旅遊服務接觸點扮演著關鍵的教育觸發機制，具體展現於六大向度：認知深化、情意共鳴、身體動作技能、科技應用熟練度、人際社群連結，以及個人生命價值的重塑。旅遊型態的抉擇，實為長者面對身心老化的主動調適策略：團體旅遊者善用高度結構化的專業後勤服務，有效降低認知與體能負荷，將精力聚焦於文化體驗與同儕互動；自由行長者則憑藉自主規劃與數位敏捷度投入「問題導向學習」，運用智慧型手機導航與即時查詢克服行程挑戰，逐步確立科技自我效能。\n\n基於服務藍圖分析，本研究提出無形服務再設計方案：觀光業者應針對高齡身心特質優化數位接觸點之介面親和度，化解數位焦慮；同時培訓第一線從業人員兼具「學習引導者」職能，在遊程各環節帶動體驗後的沉澱與反思。本研究為長壽經濟之旅遊實務指引具體轉型方向，拓展動態服務情境中非正式學習的理論對話空間。",
      "abstract_en": "Global population aging underscores the urgency of fostering healthy longevity and lifelong learning. Leisure travel offers an authentic environment where older adults pursue informal learning, sustaining psychosocial well-being and social connectedness. Nevertheless, prevailing scholarship frequently depicts older travelers as passive service recipients; it leaves unexamined the dynamic ways service touchpoints and mobile technologies catalyze self-directed inquiry. This study investigates these underlying learning mechanisms across divergent travel styles.\n\nTheoretically, this inquiry bridges adult education, drawing upon Knowles's self-directed learning, Kolb's experiential learning cycle, and McClusky's theory of margin, and service management through the analytical lens of the Service Design Blueprint (SDB). Methodologically, a qualitative multiple-case design was adopted; in-depth interviews were conducted with ten seasoned travelers aged 65 to 94. The investigation tracked and compared the experiential trajectories of Group Inclusive Tour (GIT) and Free Independent Travel (FIT) participants across pre-trip, during-trip, and post-trip phases, mapping them systematically across six primary touchpoints (including transportation, lodging, and guided activities).\n\nThe findings show that service touchpoints trigger learning in six areas: cognitive growth, emotional engagement, physical skills, technology use, social connection, and personal values. Travel mode also serves as a deliberate response to aging. Group travelers use structured services to reduce mental and physical demands, allowing them to focus on cultural experiences and social interaction. Independent travelers, by contrast, learn through problem-solving, using mobile devices for navigation and immediate solutions while building confidence in their technology skills.\n\nDrawing from these service design insights, the study advances actionable strategies for intangible service redesign: tourism providers must adapt digital interfaces to mitigate technology anxiety among older users; frontline practitioners should be equipped as learning facilitators who prompt post-trip reflection. This work delivers strategic guidance for the longevity economy while broadening theoretical frontiers on informal learning within dynamic service ecosystems.",
      "keywords": "活躍老化、非正式學習、服務設計藍圖、無形服務再設計、移動學習",
      "keywords_en": "Active Aging, Informal Learning, Service Design Blueprint, Intangible Service Redesign, Mobile Learning",
