      "title": "超高齡社會需要什麼樣的終身學習政策 從中高齡者的能力發展談起",
      "title_en": "Toward a Capability-Oriented Lifelong Learning Policy for Middle-Aged and Older Adults: Rethinking Lifelong Capability Development in a Super-Aged Society",
      "abstract": "臺灣於2025年正式進入超高齡社會，65歲以上人口已逾總人口20%。面對人口結構快速轉變，政府近年積極擴展成人學習，先後推出「第三人生大學試辦計畫」（2025）與「30+大學試辦計畫」（2026），參與學校與學習人數均快速增加。這些政策擴大了成人進入大學學習的機會，值得肯定；然而，目前政策評估仍大致著眼於課程數量、參與人次及重返勞動市場等供給與績效指標，而官方政策論述亦明確將少子化下大學招生壓力列為政策背景之一。從「第三人生大學」到「30+大學」，入學年齡由55歲降至30歲，不只是政策對象的擴大，也顯示政策仍相當重視擴充生源及回應大學機構需求。相較之下，成人學習者究竟獲得了什麼樣的實質學習機會，以及其作為學習主體的地位是否獲得充分承認，尚未成為政策設計的核心。\n\n本文以Wang（2025）所區辨的人文主義與人力資本兩種終身學習政策模型為出發點。前者重視民主公民、社會正義及全人發展，後者則將就業能力、競爭力與經濟成長置於優先位置。本文認為，人文主義模型雖在規範上較能避免將終身學習窄化為經濟工具，但其政策評估標準仍不夠明確。為補足此一不足，本文進一步提出「實質能力的擴展」（capability enhancement）作為人文主義終身學習的重要規範承諾，並以Sen與Nussbaum的能力取向為基礎，建構政策評估架構。\n\n此一架構包含三個面向：第一，實質自由的擴展，檢視學習機會是否真正增加成人選擇其有理由珍視之生活方式的可能，並關注經濟、地理等條件是否使形式上的開放無法轉化為實際可行的參與機會；第二，學習主體地位的承認，檢視政策是否尊重成人依其價值、興趣與人生安排自主選擇學習，而非將其視為維繫大學招生或補充勞動力的工具；第三，社會參與的支持，檢視學習是否有助於公民參與、代間交流及社區歸屬，而非形成另一套按年齡區隔的教育供給。\n\n以此架構檢視臺灣現行政策，至少有三項問題值得注意：其一，「30+大學試辦計畫」尚未納入依經濟需要提供的助學支持；其二，課程內容較側重職涯轉型與重返勞動市場，容易使終身學習的價值再度集中於就業與經濟產出；其三，成人學習在大學治理與行政體系中的定位仍相對邊緣。本文據此主張，未來政策應逐步擺脫以大學機構需求為主要考量的「有條件納入」，轉而承認成人學習者在大學教育中的平等地位，並使其學習機會不再主要取決於學校的招生與生存需求。如此看來，超高齡社會中的終身學習，並不只是方案設計或招生政策的問題，更涉及我們如何理解成人與高齡者作為學習者的地位。大學如何對待這群學習者，最終反映的是一個社會對人的持續發展抱持何種態度，也反映了我們希望建構一個什麼樣的社會。",
      "abstract_en": "Taiwan officially became a super-aged society in 2025, with adults aged 65 and older accounting for more than 20 percent of the total population. In response, the government has rapidly expanded its range of adult learning initiatives, from the University of the Third Life Program (2025) to the 30+ University Pilot Program (2026). These programs now encompass dozens of universities and tens of thousands of learners. Yet current policy evaluation continues to rely mainly on program-level provision and performance indicators, including course offerings, participation rates, and labor-market reintegration outcomes. Official policy rationales have also explicitly cited the enrollment pressures confronting private universities amid sustained fertility decline. This paper interprets the associated lowering of the eligibility threshold from fifty-five to thirty not simply as an adjustment to the target population, but as suggesting a policy orientation that places greater emphasis on broadening eligibility and responding to universities’ enrollment pressures than on expanding substantive learning opportunities for middle-aged and older adults and recognizing them as autonomous learning subjects. Such indicators and rationales shaped by institutional needs may be insufficient for addressing the multidimensional learning needs of middle-aged and older adults and may risk skewing policy priorities.\n\nBuilding on the two competing paradigms of lifelong learning policy identified by Wang (2025), namely, a humanistic model oriented toward democratic citizenship, social justice, and whole-person development, and a human-capital model oriented toward employability, competitiveness, and economic growth, this paper further develops capability enhancement, the third and least elaborated normative commitment of the humanistic framework, and uses it to construct a comprehensive policy-evaluation framework informed by the capability approach of Sen and Nussbaum. The paper further argues that, although the humanistic model is normatively stronger, it has historically lacked an evaluative standard that can be operationalized to offer a viable alternative to human-capital reasoning in policy discourse. The capability approach provides a more explicit analytical and normative basis for such evaluation.\n\nThree evaluative dimensions are proposed. The first, expansion of substantive freedoms, examines whether learning genuinely expands the freedom of middle-aged and older adults to pursue ways of living they have reason to value, and whether structural barriers, particularly financial exclusion and geographic inequality, limit the conversion of formal access into substantively available learning opportunities. The second, recognition of middle-aged and older adults as autonomous learning subjects, examines whether policy design respects their right to make learning choices in accordance with their own values and needs, rather than treating them as instruments of institutional survival or labor-market replenishment. The third, support for social participation, examines whether learning spaces cultivate civic engagement, intergenerational exchange, and a genuine sense of community belonging, rather than reproducing age-segregated forms of learning provision.\n\nApplied to Taiwan’s policy context, this framework identifies three institutional issues that may constrain substantive capabilities: the absence of means-tested financial aid in the 30+ Program; a curricular emphasis on career transition and labor-market reintegration, which may narrow the perceived value of learning to employment and economic outcomes; and the relatively marginal administrative position of adult learning within university governance structures. This paper calls for a shift from conditional inclusion, in which adult learners are admitted primarily in response to institutional needs, toward recognition of their equal standing as a matter of principle. In a super-aged society, how universities treat middle-aged and older learners ultimately reflects the kind of society we choose to become.",
      "keywords": "能力取向、終身學習政策、中高齡與高齡學習、超高齡社會、高齡正義",
      "keywords_en": "capability approach; lifelong learning policy; middle-aged and older adult learning; super-aged society; age justice",
      "display_presenters": "Bei-Yun Wang",
      "author_info_lines": [
        "Bei-Yun Wang",
        "Graduate Institute of Education, National Chung Cheng University",
        "Ph.D. Student",
        "bethiawang@alum.ccu.edu.tw"
      ]
