      "title": "翻轉超高齡不平等：高齡運動者健康識能對健康老化影響之實證研究",
      "title_en": "Reversing Super-Aged Inequality: An Empirical Study on the Impact of Health Literacy on Healthy Aging Among Older",
      "abstract": "全球邁入超高齡社會，促進高齡者健康老化至為關鍵。本研究旨在探討雲林縣銀髮健身俱樂部高齡運動者之健康識能與健康老化現況、人口背景差異，並評估健康識能對健康老化之預測效果，進而審視活躍高齡族群中殘存之健康不平等現象。本研究以雲林縣19處銀髮健身俱樂部高齡運動者為對象，採便利取樣，有效樣本292份。研究工具包含「個人基本資料」、「健康識能量表」及「健康老化量表」，問卷資料經SPSS統計軟體進行描述性統計、獨立樣本t檢定、ANOVA、Pearson積差相關及分層多元迴歸分析後，獲得以下六項主要結果：\n\n一、高齡運動者之健康識能程度總指標平均偏低（M = 26.69, SD = 9.67），達74.9%處於「不足」或「有限」等級。五大層面中以「理解健康資訊」（M= 34.11）表現最佳，「獲取健康資訊」（M = 21.96）最弱。\n\n二、高齡運動者之健康識能受性別（t = 2.16, p =.032）、年齡（t = 8.29, p <.001）、教育程度（F = 34.18, p <.001）及健康狀況（F = 4.53, p =.001）均呈顯著差異，其中75歲以上超高齡之健康識能顯著低於65~74歲者。\n\n三、高齡運動者之健康老化程度整體達高水準（M = 4.48, SD = 0.45），各構面中「生活品質」（M = 4.55）最佳。\n\n四、高齡運動者之健康老化受性別（t = 2.84 , p =.005，女性顯著高於男性）與健康狀況（F = 5.67, p < .001）不同而有顯著差異。\n\n五、高齡運動者之健康識能總指標與健康老化呈顯著正相關（r = .131, p = .026），尤以對「生理健康」構面之相關性最高（r = .180, p= .002）。\n\n六、控制人口背景變項後（R^= .170, p < .001），納入健康識能五個構面能顯著提升對健康老化之解釋力（△R2 = .038, F= 4.21, p< .001；總R2= .208），其中「獲取健康資訊」（β= .230, p= .021）與「溝通與互動」（β= .166, p= .037）為健康老化之顯著正向預測因子。\n\n依據上述研究結論，實證數據揭示即使在已參與俱樂部之活躍高齡族群中，高齡與資訊獲取弱勢者仍面臨結構性健康識能落差。本研究提出以下建議：\n\n一、對高齡運動者之建議:（一）提升「資訊獲取」主動性，善用多元管道與數位工具突破資訊接軌障礙。（二）強化「雙向溝通」識能，建立主動與衛教人員及專業教練互動之習慣。\n\n二、對銀髮健身俱樂部之建議:（一）推行去文字化與圖像化資訊轉譯，建構無障礙之健康資訊獲取環境。（二）實施背景分眾管理，針對超高齡（75歲）及低教育程度者提供精準輔導，弭平場域內之健康識能落差。\n\n三、對高齡教育機構與政府部門之建議:（一）推動精準化健康識能提升計畫，公平配置資源以扶助高齡與資訊雙重弱勢族群。（二）建構縱貫性追蹤評估機制，實踐以科學實證導向之健康不平等翻轉政策。",
      "abstract_en": "In the wave of a global super-aged society, promoting healthy aging among older adults is of paramount importance. This study aimed to examine the current status of health literacy and healthy aging, explore demographic differences,evaluate the predictive effect of HL on HA among older exercisers in Senior Fitness Clubs in Yunlin County, and scrutinize remaining health inequalities within active older populations. Targeting older exercisers from 19 Senior Fitness Clubs in Yunlin County, this study adopted convenience sampling and retrieved 292 valid questionnaires. Research instruments included demographic background data, a Health Literacy Scale, and a Healthy Aging Scale. Data were analyzed using SPSS for descriptive statistics, independent-samples t-tests, one-way ANOVA, Pearson product-moment correlation, and hierarchical multiple regression analysis. Six major findings were derived:\n\n1.The overall health literacy index of older exercisers was relatively low (M = 26.69, SD = 9.67), with 74.9% of participants categorized at the \"inadequate\" or \"limited\" level. Among the five dimensions, \"understanding health information\" scored the highest (M = 34.11), whereas \"accessing health information\" was the lowest (M = 21.96).\n\n2.Significant differences in health literacy were found across gender (t = 2.16, p = .032), age (t = 8.29, p < .001), educational level (F = 34.18, p < .001), and self-rated health status (F = 4.53, p = .001). Specifically, oldest-old adults (aged ≥ 75) scored significantly lower in health literacy than younger-old adults (aged 65–74).\n\n3.The overall healthy aging level of older exercisers reached a high standard (M = 4.48, SD = 0.45), with the \"quality of life\" dimension performing the best (M = 4.55).\n\n4.Healthy aging varied significantly by gender (t = 2.84, p = .005, with females scoring significantly higher than males) and self-rated health status (F = 5.67, p < .001).\n\n5.The overall health literacy index showed a significant positive correlation with healthy aging (r = .131, p = .026), exhibiting the strongest correlation with the \"physical health\" dimension (r = .180, p = .002).\n\n6.After controlling for demographic background variables (R² = .170, p < .001), adding the five dimensions of health literacy significantly improved the explanatory power for healthy aging (ΔR² = .038, F = 4.21, p < .001; total R² = .208). Among these, \"accessing health information\" (β = .230, p = .021) and \"communication and interaction\" (β = .166, p = .037) served as significant positive predictors of healthy aging.\n\nBased on these findings, empirical data reveal that even within active older populations participating in fitness clubs, individuals who are older and disadvantaged in information access still face structural health literacy gaps. This study proposes the following recommendations:\n\n1.Recommendations for Older Exercisers:\n\n(1)Enhance initiative in \"accessing information\" by utilizing diverse channels and digital tools to overcome information access barriers.\n\n(2)Strengthen \"two-way communication\" literacy by establishing the habit of actively interacting with health educators and professional trainers.\n\n2.Recommendations for Senior Fitness Clubs:\n\n(1)Implement non-textual and visual information translation to construct an accessible health information environment.\n\n(2)Practice demographic audience segmentation to provide targeted guidance for oldest-old adults (≥75 years) and those with lower educational attainment, thereby narrowing health literacy disparities within the venues.\n\n3.Recommendations for Senior Education Institutions and Government Agencies:\n\n(1)Promote targeted health literacy enhancement initiatives and allocate resources equitably to support doubly disadvantaged populations (in age and information access).\n\n(2)Establish longitudinal tracking and evaluation mechanisms to implement evidence-based policies aimed at reversing health inequalities.",
      "keywords": "高齡運動者、健康識能、健康老化、銀髮健身俱樂部、健康不平等",
      "keywords_en": "Older exercisers, Health literacy, Healthy aging, Senior fitness clubs, Health inequality",
      "display_presenters": "林玉梅",
      "author_info_lines": [
        "林玉梅｜雲林縣古坑國民中小學｜教師"
      ]
