      "title": "看見數位教學的另一道門檻：中高齡教師面臨的教學困境",
      "title_en": "Seeing Another Threshold in Digital Teaching: Teaching Challenges Faced by Middle-Aged Teachers",
      "abstract": "高齡社會已經是不可逆轉的趨勢，而數位裝置持續朝向小巧精緻發展，使中高齡者學習新科技時，需先克服視覺老化所帶來的近距離閱讀與操作挑戰。研究指出電腦視覺症候群常與長時間螢幕使用、近距離用眼及環境光線等因素有關，其症狀包含眼睛乾澀、視覺模糊及頭部緊繃或脹痛。數位課程備課高度依賴近距離視覺與長時間螢幕操作，如簡報製作、截圖辨識及手機操作示範等，對於出現視覺老化狀況之數位課程教師而言，可能增加備課及教材設計的視覺負荷，而其對教學現場的實際影響仍有待進一步探討。\n\n本研究旨在探討中高齡教師在數位課程的準備及教學歷程中所面臨的困境、工作調適、心理感受和其因應策略，以9位45至65歲具有成人數位課程教學經驗之教師為對象，進行半結構式訪談。\n\n研究結果顯示，中高齡教師的視覺困擾主要集中於備課階段，而非教學現場。長時間製作與修訂教材及辨識手機截圖，容易造成眼睛疲勞，因此多數教師會透過增加休息、放大字體、使用大尺寸螢幕及AI工具等方式降低近距離用眼負荷。相較之下，教學現場及與學員互動受影響有限，主要歸因於教師熟悉授課內容、課前充分準備及累積的教學經驗。多數教師亦未因視覺老化產生明顯心理壓力，而是透過配鏡、就醫及工作調適加以因應，但仍會擔憂未來視覺功能持續退化，因此調整備課方式，以減少長時間近距離用眼的時長和頻率。\n\n本研究可為高齡數位素養課程之師資培訓與教學支持提供實務建議，亦有助於支持更多中高齡者或退休教師投入數位課程教學，進而促進高齡族群之數位參與，並有助於縮小數位落差。",
      "abstract_en": "Population aging has become an irreversible global trend, while digital devices continue to become increasingly compact. Consequently, middle-aged and older adults face growing challenges in near-vision reading and operating digital devices when learning new technologies. Computer Vision Syndrome (CVS) is associated with prolonged screen use, sustained near work, and environmental lighting conditions, with common symptoms including dry eyes, blurred vision, and headaches. Preparing digital literacy courses requires intensive near-work activities, such as creating presentation slides, reviewing smartphone screenshots, and demonstrating smartphone operations. For middle-aged teachers experiencing age-related visual changes, these demands may increase the visual workload during lesson preparation, while their impact on classroom teaching remains unclear.\n\nThis study explored the difficulties encountered by middle-aged teachers during digital course preparation and teaching, as well as their work adaptations, psychological experiences, and coping strategies. Semi-structured interviews were conducted with nine teachers aged 45–65 who had experience teaching digital literacy courses for adults.\n\nThe findings showed that teaching-related difficulties were primarily concentrated during lesson preparation rather than classroom teaching. Prolonged preparation and revision of instructional materials, together with reviewing smartphone screenshots, frequently caused eye fatigue. Most participants reduced near-work demands by taking regular breaks, enlarging text, using larger monitors, and adopting AI tools. In contrast, classroom teaching and interactions with learners were minimally affected because instructors were familiar with the course content, prepared thoroughly, and drew on their teaching experience. Most participants reported little psychological distress related to age-related visual changes, as they believed these issues could be managed through appropriate eyewear, medical consultation, and work adaptations. However, concerns about future visual decline prompted them to modify their lesson preparation habits by reducing the duration and frequency of prolonged near-work activities.\n\nThe findings provide practical implications for teacher training and instructional support in digital literacy education for older adults. They may also encourage more middle-aged and retired teachers to engage in digital literacy instruction, thereby promoting digital participation among older adults and helping narrow the digital divide.",
      "keywords": "數位素養；視覺老化；師資培訓；電腦視覺症候群；教學準備",
      "keywords_en": "Digital Literacy; Visual Aging; Teacher Training; Computer Vision Syndrome; Teaching Preparation",
      "display_presenters": "林則豪",
      "author_info_lines": [
        "Seeing Another Threshold in Digital Teaching: Teaching Challenges Faced by Middle-Aged Teachers",
        "林則豪1、魏惠娟2",
        "1國立中正大學成人及繼續教育學系博士生",
        "2國立中正大學成人及繼續教育學系教授"
      ]
