      "title": "雲嘉地區中年人參與社區服務之實踐歷程研究",
      "title_en": "A Study on the Practical Process of Community Participation through Volunteer Service among Middle-Aged Adults in Yunlin County and Chiayi County",
      "abstract": "臺灣於2025年正式邁入超高齡社會，人口結構快速變遷已成為國家發展的重要課題，鄉村地區除面臨人口老化加劇外，亦長期受到青壯年人口外流、實際居住人口低於戶籍人口等現象影響，在此發展趨勢下，中高齡者已逐漸成為維繫鄉村社區功能的重要人力基礎，其中尤以中年人累積的專業知能及社會經驗，具備推動社區發展之潛力，同時，隨著健康老化與平均餘命的延長，多數中高齡者仍具有社會參與之能力，而中年階段則為退休前的重要準備期，若能在此階段累積社區參與經驗，可望培養參與公共事務之習慣，並作為未來高齡階段持續投入社會參與之基礎。\n\n基於上述背景，本研究旨在探討雲嘉鄉村地區中年志願工作者社區參與概況、投入動機及因應挑戰的實踐歷程，採用質性研究取向，透過文獻回顧及半結構式訪談蒐集資料，以立意取樣方式選取11位於雲林縣及嘉義縣鄉村地區服務時間超過3年，年齡介於39至55歲之中年志願工作者為研究對象，並以主題分析法進行資料編碼分析。\n\n研究結論：（一）受訪者服務場域多非其原生家鄉，顯示社區參與的場域不再侷限於傳統鄰里空間，轉向由共同理念及行動目標的組織網絡所驅動；（二）多數受訪者為首次投入社區型志願服務，投入契機主要來自工作接觸、親友引薦或服務對象邀約，以及個人內在信念的支持；（三）參與動機兼具利他、利己、社會性及情境因素，涵蓋自我實現、學習成長及傳承創新等中年階段常見的發展需求；（四）面對志願服務、家庭及工作之間的衝突時，多透過釐清角色定位、彈性調配時間或工作、安排優先順序等方式因應；（五）親友支持及服務對象的正面回饋為持續投入的重要支持力量，受訪者多認為外界質疑對其影響有限。\n\n綜上所述，本研究發現受訪者社區參與的歷程係由外在契機啟動，透過內在驅力維持，並在自我調適、心理連結與社會互動之交互作用下逐步深化，此外，本研究受訪者之參與場域呈現由地緣性社區逐漸延伸至事緣性社群的趨勢，研究結果可作為地方政府推動超高齡社會中高齡人力資源運用和社區參與政策，以及鄉村社區招募、培育與留任中年志願工作者之參考。",
      "abstract_en": "Taiwan officially entered a super-aged society in 2025, and the rapid transformation of its demographic structure has become a critical issue for national development. In addition to accelerating population aging, rural areas have been affected by the outflow of young and working-age populations, resulting in actual resident populations falling below registered populations. Within this developmental trend, middle-aged and older adults have gradually become a critical human resource foundation for supporting rural community functions. Especially, middle-aged individuals, having accumulated professional ability and social experience over the years, are especially well-positioned as potential drivers of community development. Meanwhile, with healthy aging and prolonged life expectancy, most middle-aged and older adults keep the capacity for social participation. Middle adulthood, as a preparatory stage before retirement, offers a critical opportunity to cultivate habits of civic engagement through community participation, thereby laying the foundation for sustained participation in later life.\n\nAgainst this backdrop, this study aims to explore the status, initial motivations, and the practical process of overcoming challenges on community participation among middle-aged volunteer workers in rural areas of Yunlin County and Chiayi County. Adopting a qualitative research approach, data were collected through literature review and semi-structured interviews. There are 11 middle-aged volunteer workers, aged between 39 to 55 had served for over three years in rural areas and were selected as research participants of Yunlin County and Chiayi County. Thematic analysis was employed for data coding and interpretation.\n\nThe findings reveal: (1) The service sites of most interviewees were not their places of origin or hometown communities indicating that the spaces for community participation are no longer confined to traditional neighborhoods but are instead driven by organizational networks with shared philosophies and action goals; (2) Most interviewees participated in community-based volunteer service for the first time, with their initial engagement primarily triggered by triggered by external opportunities such as work-related contact, referrals from relatives and friends, or invitations from service recipients, as well as supported by personal intrinsic beliefs ; (3) Participation motivations encompassed altruistic, egoistic, social, and situational factors, covering common developmental needs in middle adulthood, such as self-actualization, self-improvement, as well as generativity and innovation; (4) When facing conflicts between volunteer service, family, and work, interviewees mostly coped by clarifying role positioning, flexibly allocating time or work, and prioritizing tasks; (5) Support from family and friends, along with positive feedback from service recipients, served as vital sustaining forces for continuous involvement; most interviewees perceived external skepticism as having limited impact on them.\n\nIn summary, this study finds that the practical process of community participation is started by external opportunities, sustained by intrinsic drives, and progressively deepened through the interaction of self-adjustment, psychological connection, and social interaction. Furthermore, participants' involvement patterns reveal a gradual shift from geographic community toward community of interest. These insights provide valuable implications for local governments in using middle-aged human resources within super-aged societies, as well as for rural communities looking to recruit, cultivate, and keep middle-aged volunteer workers.",
      "keywords": "社區參與、志願服務、成年中期、實踐歷程",
      "keywords_en": "Community Participation; Volunteer Service; Middle Age; Practical Processes.",
      "display_presenters": "余金娥",
